[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe problem:[22m The Rust translation of [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m was functionally correct — the hashing logic, buffer layout, and blake256 call all matched the C original. However, the test runner loads the compiled Rust library ([38;5;10mlibblake.so[0m) dynamically and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m via [38;5;10mdlsym[0m. This symbol was never exported.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mWhy [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m?[22m In the C code, [38;5;10mhash.h[0m uses a namespace macro:[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m#define prf_addr SPX_NAMESPACE(prf_addr)
[0m[0m[0m
where [38;5;10mSPX_NAMESPACE(s)[0m expands to [38;5;10mSPX_##s[0m. So the C compiler sees the function as [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m in the final binary. The Rust translation had [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m as an internal Rust function but never created a corresponding [38;5;10m#[no_mangle] extern "C"[0m export with the [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m name.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe fix:[22m Added a single FFI wrapper in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn SPX_prf_addr(
    out: *mut c_uchar,
    ctx: *const context::SpxCtx,
    addr: *const [u32; 8],
) {
    let ctx_r = unsafe { &*ctx };
    let addr_r = unsafe { &*addr };
    let out_s = unsafe { std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(out, params::SPX_N) };
    hash_blake::prf_addr(out_s, ctx_r, addr_r);
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This bridges the C ABI expectation (the [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m symbol with raw pointer arguments) to the safe Rust [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m implementation. After this change, all test vectors pass (1/1).[0m[0m
